  {:title
   "import-from-file's post-import count verification is a tautology — it recomputes counts from the payload, not from the store",
   :file "src/epiphany/domain/backup.clj:162-169",
   :detail
   "`imported-counts` is `(into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [k (count v)]) data))` — derived from the same `data` that validate-backup-payload already proved matches the manifest (lines 89-101). The 'Imported collection counts do not match backup manifest' check can therefore never fail and verifies nothing about what actually landed. Demonstrated: I seeded an export with 1 repository-location record, then called import-from-file against a port whose :import-all silently drops everything — import-from-file returned `{\"repository-location\" 1, ...}` (success) while `(export-all target)` showed all collections empty. Combined with the mongo import-all blanket catch above, a partially-failed Mongo import is reported as fully successful with no signal. Either re-read counts via :export-all after import or delete the dead check.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017f",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim is technically accurate that the check at backup.clj:163-169 is vacuous (it recomputes counts from the same `data` that validate-backup-payload already proved equal to the manifest at lines 89-101, so it can never fail), but it does not gate promotion. (1) The vacuous check is redundant dead code, not incorrect behavior: no acceptance criterion, invariant, or verification row of ENG-017F requires post-import store re-reads — the card's scope is pre-mutation manifest/record validation, which is delivered and tested with 7 corruption fixtures and state-unchanged proofs. (2) Meaningful post-import verification of what actually landed already exists where the architecture puts it: restore-drill (backup.clj:195-232) re-exports via :export-all and compares content-hashes against the original export — exactly the reviewer's suggested remedy — and that export→import→export round trip is covered by integration tests against both in-memory and real Mongo adapters. The card's non-goals explicitly defer full restore-drill scope to ENG-021A. (3) The 'demonstrated' silent-success scenario requires a deliberately non-conforming port whose :import-all drops everything; neither shipped adapter does that (in-memory imports fully; mongo only swallows MongoWriteException for idempotent re-insert parity with in-memory's duplicate-request-id skip). The genuine residual risk in that combined scenario — mongo import-all's blanket catch — is a separate finding the reviewer filed independently; this tautology adds no exploitable behavior beyond it. Removing the dead guard is a cosmetic cleanup, not a review→done blocker."
    "The factual observation is accurate — backup.clj:163 recomputes imported-counts from the same `data` map that validate-backup-payload (lines 89-101) already proved equals the manifest, so the check at 164-169 can never fail and is dead code. But it does not gate this card's promotion. (1) The card's contract is explicitly pre-mutation validation: Scope says import-from-file 'validates all of it **before** any mutation', and its Invariants require only that a failing backup mutates nothing — all delivered and test-pinned (7 corruption fixtures with state-unchanged proof). (2) Whether data actually landed is verified by the card's own canonical export→import→export round-trip test run against real Mongo (integration row ✓ per the IMPLEMENTED comment), not by this redundant runtime check; the reviewer's failure demonstration required a deliberately sabotaged fake port whose :import-all silently drops everything, whereas the real mongo and in-memory adapters both validate the entire payload upfront before any insert (per the delivered comment and mongo import-all's pre-insert validation). (3) The suggested remedy — re-read via :export-all after import — is precisely what restore-drill does (backup.clj:195-232, step 4 re-export + content-hash comparison), and the card's Non-goals explicitly defer the restore drill to ENG-021A, which is implemented (commit d90188a). (4) The claimed severity is 'should-fix', and the dead check is harmless redundancy rather than a signal-loss defect in any real adapter path; the 'combined with the mongo blanket catch' escalation belongs to that separate finding, not this one. Dead-but-harmless code plus an out-of-scope remedy does not block review→done; a cleanup comment or follow-up chore suffices."]}
  {:title
   "Manifest :collections and :content-hash checks are optional — stripped-hash backups import with zero tamper evidence",
   :file "src/epiphany/domain/backup.clj",
   :detail
   "validate-backup-payload guards the collection/count check with `(when (:collections manifest) ...)` (line 91) and the content-hash check with `(when (:content-hash manifest) ...)` (line 102). A backup file whose manifest simply omits :content-hash (and/or :collections) skips those checks entirely: only format, version, and per-record schema validation remain. Demonstrable scenario: export a backup, dissoc :content-hash from the manifest, edit a record's :path/raw string to an attacker-chosen but schema-valid value, and import-from-file succeeds with no integrity finding — the exact hand-edit the hash exists to detect. The card's Scope says the manifest contract is 'format/version, expected collections, per-collection counts, and a SHA-256 of the canonical payload; import-from-file validates all of it before any mutation', and its Risks section explicitly directs 'a documented one-time re-export path rather than a permissive legacy branch' — the conditional checks are that permissive branch. Notably the new tests themselves depend on this bypass (schema-violating-record and unknown-record-version fixtures strip :content-hash to isolate their failure mode), so tightening it requires adjusting those fixtures to recompute the hash instead of omitting it.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017f",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The factual description of the code is accurate — validate-backup-payload does guard both checks with `when`, and the integrity-test fixtures do dissoc :content-hash — but the claimed impact is overstated and this is not promotion-gating. (1) The content-hash is a self-contained, unsigned SHA-256 over `pr-str` of the payload: any actor who can edit the backup file can recompute the hash with the same two-line function the exporter uses, so making the check mandatory changes nothing against the claimed deliberate-tamper scenario — 'dissoc the hash' and 'recompute the hash' are equally trivial to the same attacker. The hash's real function (per its own docstring) is detecting accidental corruption, and corruption severe enough to matter is already caught by the EDN parse check (truncation) or by the hash itself when present; corruption that surgically removes only the :content-hash field while leaving parseable EDN and schema-valid records is contrived. (2) Every backup the card's own exporter produces always contains both :collections and :content-hash, so the delivered path is fully validated and all the card's scoped verification rows pass — flip-checksum, edited-count, truncate, and version-bump fixtures all fail pre-mutation with state-unchanged proof (backup_integrity_test.clj, 723 unit tests green per the completion record). (3) The residual exposure is a hardening nit (reject manifests missing required manifest keys, or adjust the two fixtures to recompute the hash), worth a follow-up comment or small card, but it does not violate any acceptance criterion as written — each criterion speaks to corruption fixtures failing pre-mutation, which they demonstrably do — and it does not gate a review→done promotion."
    "Verified against src/epiphany/domain/backup.clj (from this card's own commit 5d9f9e8, confirmed newest commit touching the file): validate-backup-payload line 91 guards the collections/count check with `(when (:collections manifest) ...)` and line 102 guards the content-hash check with `(when (:content-hash manifest) ...)`; the post-import count check in import-from-file (line 164) is likewise conditional. Since export-to-file (lines 139-143) always writes both keys, the only manifests lacking them are hand-edited ones — and the claimed attack (dissoc :content-hash, edit a record's :path/raw to a schema-valid value, import succeeds with no integrity finding) is real: validate-record would pass a schema-valid edit and no hash check runs. The card's own test fixtures depend on the bypass: schema-violating-record-is-corrupt (backup_integrity_test.clj:110-127) and unknown-record-version (lines 129-140) both `(dissoc manifest :content-hash)` to isolate their failure mode, proving the bypass is load-bearing. No card comment documents this as an accepted tradeoff; the IMPLEMENTED comment claims 'content-hash... all validated BEFORE any mutation' without qualification. The card's Scope declares 'import-from-file validates all of it before any mutation' and its Risks section explicitly forbids 'a permissive legacy branch' in favor of a documented re-export path — the conditional checks are precisely that permissive branch, and they silently nullify the tamper evidence that is the card's core deliverable. The finding is factually accurate, in-scope, undocumented-as-accepted, and strikes at the central integrity guarantee of an integrity card; it survives the skeptic pass."]}
  {:title
   "validate-record is a law-category validator placed in domain/, making both infra adapters require a domain namespace",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/adapters/mongo.clj",
   :detail
   "validate-record uses only epiphany.law.operations/collection-schemas and epiphany.law.registry/explain — pure contract enforcement, i.e. exactly the law/ quadrant's charter ('Malli schemas, validators, guards. No I/O. Contracts only.'). Placing it in domain/backup forced infra adapters to depend on a domain namespace: mongo.clj:16 and in_memory.clj:18 both added `[epiphany.domain.backup :as backup]`, pulling domain backup policy (manifest version, file format, io requires) into the infra decode path — infra quadrant rule is 'No domain policy.' The card records the sharing as deliberate, and law/operations' new collection-schemas itself is correctly placed (pure EDN contract data), so this is a placement observation, not a functional defect.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017f",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The placement was deliberate and documented, and the claimed rule violation misreads the quadrant contract. (1) Documented: the card's IMPLEMENTED comment explicitly records 'domain/backup: validate-record + validate-backup-payload' as delivered scope, and validate-record's own docstring states 'Shared by backup import and adapter decode' — the cross-adapter sharing is a recorded design decision, not an accident. (2) Rule misread: the infra quadrant rule 'No domain policy' governs namespace *contents* (infra code must not contain domain policy), not the dependency graph. infra→domain is the canonical hexagonal dependency direction (adapters depend on domain/law contracts); law/ports.clj itself references epiphany.domain.backup in its op documentation, so the codebase already treats this direction as legitimate. Infra adapters calling a pure validator does not import backup policy (manifest version, file I/O, restore-drill) into the decode path — only the pure validate-record function is invoked, and nothing in mongo.clj or in_memory.clj references the policy parts of the namespace. (3) Grandfathered context: domain/backup predates this card (the card's own decision-context cites backup.clj:81-108 as pre-existing defects); the card extended the existing namespace rather than creating a new layering violation. (4) Even taking the placement preference at face value, the reviewer concedes it is 'a placement observation, not a functional defect' at nit severity — moving one pure function from domain/ to law/ is a cosmetic refactor that does not gate a review→done promotion of a card whose acceptance criteria (corruption fixtures, integrity findings, round trip, outcome distinctness) are all demonstrated. Default-to-refute applies: not promotion-gating."
    "Factually the placement is as described, but the finding is self-refuting as a promotion gate. (1) The claim's own text concedes 'the card records the sharing as deliberate' and 'this is a placement observation, not a functional defect' — validate-record's docstring in domain/backup.clj:39-42 explicitly documents it as 'Shared by backup import and adapter decode', and the card's IMPLEMENTED comment (commit 5d9f9e8) records this sharing as a delivered design choice. (2) Severity is claimed as 'nit'; a nit-level namespace-placement observation with no functional defect cannot gate a review→done promotion. (3) Infra→domain requires are already the established pattern in this repo — infra/workbench.clj requires nine epiphany.domain.* namespaces and infra/main.clj requires ten, so the quadrant rule is applied in practice with composition/wiring exceptions; two adapters sharing one pure validator (which depends only on law/operations and law/registry, no I/O) is consistent with that de facto architecture. (4) Moving validate-record into law/ would be wrong in the other direction: law/ is 'contracts only', and validate-record enforces backup-specific policy (expected-record-version, collection vocabulary, integrity ex-info categories), which is domain policy by the repo's own definitions. Duplicating it per-adapter would violate DRY and invite drift between the two adapters' decode validation — exactly what ENG-017F's mutation-safety parity tests guard against. No action warranted."]}
  {:title
   "'Four outcomes stay distinct' test does not deliver the verification row's 'one test per pair'",
   :file "test/epiphany/domain/backup_integrity_test.clj",
