   "The card's verification table promises 'One test per pair that must not collapse'. integrity-outcomes-are-pairwise-distinct instead asserts `(= 4 (count categories))` on a literal set of four keywords and two tautological `(not= :integrity/corrupt :integrity/unsupported-version)` checks that cannot fail, plus one behavioral check (empty backup imports cleanly). Behavioral non-collapse is in substance covered by the surrounding fixtures (each corruption fixture asserts its specific :code, missing-file asserts :source/unavailable), so the outcome is sound — but the named test is ceremonial and does not match the evidence the row claims.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017f",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim is factually accurate about the test's content (backup_integrity_test.clj:163-175 does assert `(= 4 (count categories))` on a literal keyword set plus two trivially-true `not=` checks), but the reviewer themselves classified it as a 'nit' and conceded the substantive behavior is covered: each of the seven corruption fixtures asserts its specific `:code` with state-unchanged proof (lines 130-158), `missing-file-is-source-unavailable` asserts `:source/unavailable`, and the empty-backup behavioral check inside the named test does exercise the empty-vs-error path through real export/import. The card's verification row 'Four outcomes stay distinct' is thus met in substance by the fixture suite; the named test is a vocabulary-pinning guard (it would fail if anyone collapsed or renamed an outcome keyword in the set), not ceremonial dead weight. A self-labeled nit about test naming/structure, with the underlying behavior independently proven elsewhere, is too minor to gate a review→done promotion."
    "The claim is factually accurate about the test's construction — `(= 4 (count categories))` on a literal four-keyword set and `(not= :integrity/corrupt :integrity/unsupported-version)` on distinct literals are compile-time truths that cannot fail, so integrity-outcomes-are-pairwise-distinct does not literally deliver 'one test per pair.' But the claim self-classifies as a nit and explicitly concedes the outcome is sound: behavioral non-collapse is genuinely covered by the surrounding fixtures (each corruption fixture asserts its specific :code — :integrity/corrupt vs :integrity/unsupported-version vs :source/unavailable — and the empty-backup check proves empty imports without error), so the verification row's substance (four outcomes stay distinct, unit-level evidence) is delivered and the card's completion comment records that row passing. The gap is only between the row's phrasing ('one test per pair') and the test layout, not in the verified behavior. A literal six-pair test matrix asserting keyword inequality would add zero failure-catching power. Per the skeptic standard a surviving finding must be real, in-scope, AND promotion-gating; a self-admitted nit with no behavioral consequence is not promotion-gating, so it is refuted."]}
  {:title
   "Unreadable-but-existing backup file collapses :source/unavailable into :integrity/corrupt",
   :file "src/epiphany/domain/backup.clj",
   :detail
   "read-backup-file (lines 122-129) wraps `slurp` inside the same try as `edn/read-string` and maps any Exception to {:code :integrity/corrupt, message 'not parseable EDN (truncated or corrupted)'}. A file that exists but cannot be read — permission denied, or the path is a directory — throws IOException from slurp and is reported as :integrity/corrupt, while the namespace's own outcome vocabulary (lines 9-17) and the card reserve :source/unavailable for 'a named source cannot be read.' ADR-004 decision 6 / required invariants: 'unavailable, corrupt ... outcomes must remain distinct.' Scenario: `ep import-from-file` against a root-owned backup file → operator is told the file is corrupt/truncated and may re-export needlessly, when the real fault is availability. Fix: catch java.io.IOException from the slurp separately and classify it :source/unavailable; reserve :integrity/corrupt for edn parse failures. Only the missing-file branch (line 118-121) currently produces :source/unavailable.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017f",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives true,
   :reasons
   ["The claim is factually verified against the code: read-backup-file (backup.clj:122-129) places `slurp` inside the same try as `edn/read-string` and catches bare Exception, so an IOException from slurp (permission denied, path-is-a-directory — both cases where .exists returns true and bypass the :source/unavailable branch at 118-121) is rethrown as {:code :integrity/corrupt} with the message 'not parseable EDN (truncated or corrupted)'. Only nonexistence produces :source/unavailable. The namespace's own outcome vocabulary (lines 9-17) defines :source/unavailable as 'a named source cannot be read' — which precisely describes a permission-denied or directory path, not a parse failure. This is in-scope: the card's Scope explicitly lists 'Distinct outcome data for: :integrity/corrupt, :integrity/unsupported-version, :source/unavailable' and cites ADR-004 decision 6 that these outcomes 'must remain distinguishable'; the misclassification collapses two of the four named outcomes the card exists to keep distinct. The card's IMPLEMENTED comment documents missing-file vs corrupt but does not record unreadable-but-existing as an accepted tradeoff, so it is not documented-deliberate. It is promotion-gating at should-fix severity: the fix is a one-line separate catch of java.io.IOException, the error message actively misleads an operator toward a needless re-export (the card itself flags re-export as a costly, documented-risk path), and the card's 'four outcomes stay distinct' verification row is only partially honored as delivered."
    "The claim is factually verified against the code. read-backup-file (backup.clj:122-129) places `slurp` inside the same try as `edn/read-string` and catches bare Exception, rethrowing everything as {:code :integrity/corrupt} with the message 'not parseable EDN (truncated or corrupted)'. Only the .exists check at 118-121 produces :source/unavailable; .exists returns true for permission-denied files and directories, so an IOException from slurp (FileNotFoundException 'Permission denied' or 'Is a directory') is misclassified as :integrity/corrupt. This is not grandfathered code: the card's own IMPLEMENTED comment (commit 5d9f9e8) claims ownership of read-backup-file's behavior. It is in-scope: the card's Intent and Scope exist precisely to make 'corrupt / unsupported-version / unavailable / empty four distinct, non-collapsible outcomes' under ADR-004 decision 6, and the namespace docstring (lines 9-17) defines :source/unavailable as 'a named source cannot be read' — exactly the permission-denied case. The delivered 'four outcomes stay distinct' test is nearly tautological (backup_integrity_test.clj:168 asserts (not= :source/unavailable :integrity/corrupt), comparing two keywords), so the verification row does not actually cover this collapse. No card comment documents the unreadable-but-existing case as an accepted tradeoff — the IMPLEMENTED comment documents only missing-vs-unparseable. Not too minor to gate: the misclassification contradicts the card's central invariant, downstream consumers (ENG-021A drill, ENG-017I epistemic laws) dispatch on :code not on the buried :parse-error message, and the fix is a one-line separate catch of java.io.IOException. The finding survives."]}
  {:title
   "Manifest count/hash checks are optional — a tampered backup can bypass them by deleting the fields (permissive legacy branch the card's own risk section forbids)",
   :file "src/epiphany/domain/backup.clj",
   :detail
   "validate-backup-payload guards the collection-set/count check with `(when (:collections manifest) ...)` (line 91) and the content-hash check with `(when (:content-hash manifest) ...)` (line 102). The card scope states the manifest contract IS 'format/version, expected collections, per-collection counts, and a SHA-256 of the canonical payload' and that import-from-file 'validates all of it before any mutation'; the card's risk section explicitly says 'define a documented one-time re-export path rather than a permissive legacy branch.' These when-guards are exactly a permissive branch: a hand-edited backup that remains schema-valid but alters record content (e.g. rewrites a :repository/path string) sails through if the editor simply dissocs :content-hash and :collections from the manifest. The commit's own fixtures rely on the loophole: test/epiphany/domain/backup_integrity_test.clj lines 120-123 and 136 dissoc :content-hash precisely so the hash check won't fire before the schema/version check under test. export-to-file always writes both fields (backup.clj:139-143), so making them required breaks no legitimate backup. Violates ADR-004 decision 6 (integrity constraints are gates, not optional inputs) and the card's stated invariant that every manifest check runs pre-mutation.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017f",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives true,
   :reasons
   ["The claim survives every refutation angle. (1) Factually correct: src/epiphany/domain/backup.clj lines 91 and 102 do guard the collections/count and content-hash checks with `(when (:collections manifest) ...)` and `(when (:content-hash manifest) ...)`; export-to-file (lines 139-143) unconditionally writes both fields, so no legitimate backup ever omits them; and test/epiphany/domain/backup_integrity_test.clj lines 120-123 and 136 do dissoc :content-hash so the fixture under test (schema/version violation) fires rather than the hash check — the tests literally depend on the guard being skippable. A hand-edited backup that dissocs :content-hash and :collections and rewrites a schema-valid field (e.g. a :repository/path string) passes validate-backup-payload, since per-record schema checks cannot catch a string-to-string rewrite. (2) Not deliberate or documented: no card comment, commit message, or design doc records accepting optional manifest fields; the card's risk section explicitly says 'define a documented one-time re-export path rather than a permissive legacy branch,' and the IMPLEMENTED comment overclaims that 'every manifest check ... runs BEFORE any mutation.' (3) In scope and not grandfathered: validate-backup-payload is entirely new in the card's own commit 5d9f9e8, and the backup manifest contract ('format/version, expected collections, per-collection counts, and a SHA-256 ... import-from-file validates all of it') is the card's core scope item. Since manifest version 1 is the only version ever written and export always emits both fields, the when-guards serve no compatibility purpose — they are precisely the permissive branch the card forbids, and they contradict ADR-004 decision 6 ('integrity constraints are gates, not weighted-score inputs'). The fix is trivial (require the fields, reject when absent), so this is a real, in-scope, promotion-gating finding against an adversarial-integrity card whose central invariant it violates."
    "Verified against the code, the card, and ADR-004; the claim is factually accurate, in-scope, and not documented as an accepted tradeoff. (1) Code: backup.clj:91 and :102 do guard the collections/count and content-hash checks with `(when (:collections manifest) ...)` / `(when (:content-hash manifest) ...)`, so a manifest that simply omits those keys skips both checks entirely; validate-backup-payload only hard-requires :format and :version. (2) The scenario is real: per-record schema validation (lines 109-111) still runs, but the claim's example — rewriting a :repository/path string to another schema-valid string — passes record validation, so with :content-hash and :collections dissoc'd the tampered payload imports silently. The sha256-base64 docstring (lines 26-27) explicitly states its purpose is 'to detect a corrupted or hand-edited backup file,' a purpose the when-guard defeats. (3) The card's Scope text says the manifest contract IS 'format/version, expected collections, per-collection counts, and a SHA-256 of the canonical payload' and that import-from-file 'validates all of it before any mutation'; its Risks section explicitly prescribes 'a documented one-time re-export path rather than a permissive legacy branch' — these when-guards are precisely such a permissive branch, and no card comment records it as an accepted tradeoff (the IMPLEMENTED comment claims all checks run pre-mutation without noting the optionality). (4) The commit's own fixtures exploit the loophole: backup_integrity_test.clj:120-123 and :136 dissoc :content-hash rather than recomputing it so the hash check cannot fire before the schema/version check under test. (5) export-to-file (backup.clj:139-143) unconditionally writes both fields, so requiring them breaks no legitimately produced backup. ADR-004 decision 6 ('non-negotiable integrity constraints are gates, not weighted-score inputs') supports the gating characterization: a check bypassable by deleting its input is not a gate. Fix is small (require the fields; make the two fixtures recompute the hash), but the finding is a genuine contradiction of the card's stated contract and risk directive, so it stands."]}
  {:title
   "ex-data for malformed non-map payloads contains a lazy (keys payload) that throws on realization",
   :file "src/epiphany/domain/backup.clj",
   :detail
   "validate-backup-payload line 79 puts `:payload-keys (keys payload)` into the ex-data. For a parseable-but-non-map payload (e.g. a file containing `[1 2 3]`), the named :integrity/corrupt outcome IS produced correctly (verified in a REPL), but (keys <vector>) returns a lazy seq that throws ClassCastException ('Long cannot be cast to Map$Entry') when realized — e.g. when CLI/reporting code pr-strs the ex-data of the integrity finding. One-line guard (only include :payload-keys when (map? payload)) removes the latent crash in the error-reporting path of a card whose purpose is trustworthy error reporting.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017f",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The mechanic is real — verified in a REPL that (keys [1 2 3]) yields a lazy seq throwing ClassCastException on realization — but the finding does not survive as promotion-gating. (1) The card's actual contract holds: the named :integrity/corrupt outcome IS produced correctly for a parseable-but-non-map payload, as the reviewer concedes; the defect lives only in an auxiliary diagnostic field of the ex-data. (2) No delivered code path realizes the value: import-from-file is not wired to any CLI subcommand (grep of main.clj shows no backup/import command; the CLI error formatters like git-boundary-error print only selected ex-data keys, never pr-str the whole map), and restore-drill — the only caller — explicitly belongs to ENG-021A's scope per the card's Non-goals. The crash is therefore latent in hypothetical future reporting code, not in anything this card shipped. (3) The reviewer self-classifies severity as 'nit', and the skeptic mandate is to refute findings too minor to gate review→done. The one-line (map? payload) guard is a reasonable polish suggestion for a follow-up comment on the card, not a defect blocking promotion of a card whose verification rows (7 corruption fixtures with pre-mutation proof, 4-outcome distinctness, round-trip) all pass."
    "The mechanism is technically real (verified in a REPL: `(keys [1 2 3])` is a lazy seq whose realization via pr-str throws ClassCastException, and `ExceptionInfo.toString`/`str` of the exception would also throw), but the claimed impact scenario does not occur anywhere in the delivered code. Every ex-data consumer in the repo reads selectively: the CLI handlers in infra/main.clj (lines 126-134, 239-245, 595-603, 675-681, 728-732) use `(.getMessage e)` plus keyword lookups `(:code data)`, `(:hint data)`, `(:git-error data)` — keyword lookups on a hash map do not realize sibling lazy values, and `.getMessage` returns the plain message (verified: returns \"x\" with no throw). The HTTP handler (infra/http.clj:171-181, log-server-error! at 151-154) likewise uses only `(:code data)` and `(.getMessage e)`, never pr-str of the ex-data. So the named :integrity/corrupt outcome surfaces correctly through every actual reporting path; the latent crash only manifests if hypothetical future code pr-strs the whole ex-data map. Moreover the card explicitly scopes itself as providing integrity primitives to ENG-021A ('Provides to ENG-021A: manifest validation + integrity outcomes its restore drill must consume'); no restore/backup command is wired into main.clj or http.clj in this card's diff. The finding is a self-described nit about a one-key guard against a consumer that does not exist in delivered scope — not promotion-gating."]}
  {:title
   "Future :format keyword is classified :integrity/corrupt while the exception message says 'Unsupported'",
   :file "src/epiphany/domain/backup.clj",
   :detail
   "validate-backup-payload lines 80-83: a well-formed backup stamped :epiphany-backup-v2 (a future format, the exact analog of the bumped manifest :version fixture) throws {:code :integrity/corrupt} with message 'Unsupported backup format' — code and message disagree, and the adjacent manifest :version check (lines 84-88) correctly uses :integrity/unsupported-version for the same future-version situation. The card's outcome vocabulary exists so operators can distinguish 're-export the file' (corrupt) from 'upgrade/downgrade epiphany' (unsupported-version); this misclassification collapses that distinction for the format dimension. The new 4-outcome distinctness test (backup_integrity_test.clj:163-175) never exercises the format branch, so nothing pins the category.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017f",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The code reading is accurate — validate-backup-payload (backup.clj:80-83) does throw {:code :integrity/corrupt} with message 'Unsupported backup format' for a non-v1 :format, while the adjacent manifest :version check uses :integrity/unsupported-version. But the finding is refuted on gating grounds, not factual ones: (1) The reviewer self-classifies this as a 'nit', and the skeptic charter explicitly refutes findings too minor to gate a review→done promotion. (2) The claimed operational harm — collapsing the corrupt vs unsupported-version distinction — does not actually materialize: the ex-info message literally says 'Unsupported backup format' and the ex-data carries the offending :format value, so an operator inspecting the failure gets exactly the 'upgrade/downgrade epiphany' signal; only the programmatic :code keyword is arguably miscategorized. (3) Whether an unrecognized :format keyword is even the 'exact analog' of a bumped integer :version is debatable — a format tag outside the known enumeration is reasonably classified as a malformed/unrecognized payload shape (:integrity/corrupt covers 'malformed payload' per the ns docstring), whereas :version is the explicitly versioned dimension the card's fixtures exercise. (4) The card's delivered scope and verification rows (7 corruption fixtures including bumped manifest version, 4-outcome distinctness, pre-mutation safety) are all met and test-pinned; no acceptance criterion requires the format branch to use :integrity/unsupported-version, and no comment history documents it as a defect. A one-line keyword swap is a legitimate follow-up polish item, not a promotion blocker."
    "The claim is factually accurate about the code (backup.clj:80-83 does throw :integrity/corrupt with message 'Unsupported backup format' while the adjacent version check at :84-88 uses :integrity/unsupported-version), but it does not survive as a promotion-gating finding. (1) The reviewer themselves classifies it as a nit — a code/message wording mismatch, not a behavioral defect: the import still fails pre-mutation with a named, non-collapsed integrity category, satisfying the card's scope ('format/version' manifest contract, 'Distinct outcome data' for the four outcomes) and every acceptance criterion. (2) The classification is defensible by design: the format keyword is a closed vocabulary tag, not an ordinal version. A manifest stamped anything other than :epiphany-backup-v1 includes the case 'this is not an epiphany backup at all' (wrong file, garbage), for which :integrity/corrupt is the correct tier — it is not purely the 'future version' analog the claim assumes. (3) The docstring at lines 70-74 explicitly documents the category boundaries as delivered ('corrupt — malformed shape...'; 'unsupported-version — unknown manifest or record version'), and the card's IMPLEMENTED comment records exactly the delivered fixture set (bumped manifest version, unknown record version, etc.) with all verification rows green and kondo clean. (4) The 4-outcome distinctness test the claim cites covers the four categories the card's Scope names; pinning the format branch's category was never an acceptance criterion. At most this warrants a one-line message tweak or docstring note in a follow-up; it does not gate review→done."]}
  {:title
   "read-backup-file extends direct file I/O inside the domain quadrant",
   :file "src/epiphany/domain/backup.clj",
   :detail
   "AGENTS.md namespace law: domain/ is 'Pure. Zero I/O.' backup.clj already violated this before 5d9f9e8 (export-to-file spits, old import-from-file slurped — pre-existing, out of scope), but this commit adds new I/O surface in domain rather than routing reads through an injected port: `(.exists (io/file file-path))` and `(slurp file-path)` at lines 118-123. The validation core (validate-record, validate-backup-payload) is genuinely pure and well-placed; only the file-touching shim needed to live behind a port or in infra. Flagging strictly the new code per review scope.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017f",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["Factually accurate but not promotion-gating. Git history confirms domain/backup.clj was born with direct file I/O: pre-5d9f9e8 it already contained export-to-file (io/make-parents + spit) and restore-drill (edn/read-string + slurp) — the namespace was established as a file-level backup orchestration namespace in commits d90188a/6924b10, not by this card. The new read-backup-file shim (6 lines of .exists/slurp wrapped in named ex-info outcomes) follows that file's grandfathered convention and exists solely to serve import-from-file, which was always an I/O entry point. The card's actual scope was validation of decoded/imported data, and the claim itself concedes that the scoped deliverables (validate-record, validate-backup-payload) are 'genuinely pure and well-placed' in domain/law. Moving the read shim behind a port or into infra would require restructuring a pre-existing namespace boundary — explicitly outside ENG-017F's scope (Scope section covers validation, manifest contract, and outcome distinctness; no namespace reorganization). The claim is self-labeled a 'nit' by the reviewer; a nit extending a documented pre-existing pattern, where all card-owned code is pure, cannot gate review→done. If the namespace law violation matters, it warrants a separate chore card to migrate all of backup.clj's I/O, not a gate on this card."
    "The claim is factually accurate about the code (read-backup-file at backup.clj:114-129 does call .exists/slurp in domain/), but it refutes itself as promotion-gating on three counts. (1) Self-declared severity: the reviewer labels this a 'nit' — nits do not gate a review→done promotion under the skeptic standard. (2) Consistency with the file's established pattern: the claim itself concedes that export-to-file already spits, restore-drill already slurps/edn-reads (lines 145-146, 225), and import-from-file already did file I/O before commit 5d9f9e8. The new private read shim is not a new architectural direction; it matches the pre-existing, grandfathered style of the very file the card was scoped to harden. Routing a 15-line EDN-read helper behind an injected port while its siblings export-to-file/restore-drill do direct I/O would create inconsistency, not purity. (3) Scope: the card's delivered scope (per its IMPLEMENTED comment and Scope section) was manifest/payload validation, integrity outcome taxonomy, and the collection-name mapping — all delivered with pure validation cores (validate-record, validate-backup-payload), which the reviewer acknowledges are 'genuinely pure and well-placed.' A wholesale domain-quadrant purity refactor of backup.clj is architectural cleanup that belongs on its own card, not a gate on this one. Nothing in the card's comment history records a promise to purify the file's I/O; the finding is a real-but-minor style observation, correctly tagged nit, and not worth blocking promotion."]}
  {:title
   "Mongo :import-all swallows every MongoWriteException, so failed inserts are silently dropped and the import reports success",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/adapters/mongo.clj:789-792",
   :detail
   "The import insert loop ends with `(catch MongoWriteException _e nil)` — it discards ALL write-failure codes, unlike every other write path in the same file which checks `(= 11000 (.getCode e))` before treating a dup-key as idempotent (lines 608-619, 629-635). Any non-duplicate-key write failure during import (driver-side document validation, oversized doc, auth/write-concern error) is silently swallowed: the record is lost and `:import-all` returns normally. A dup `_id` with materially different content is likewise silently kept. This is exactly the silent-failure class ENG-017F exists to eliminate on the import path, and it is undetectable from the outside because of the next finding.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017f",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["Verified against mongo.clj:789-792: the :import-all insert loop ends with (catch MongoWriteException _e nil), swallowing every write-failure code with no (= 11000 (.getCode e)) dup-key check — unlike insert-or-idempotent! (608-619) and validated-insert! (629-635) in the same file, which only treat code 11000 as idempotent and route everything else to storage-error!. The in-memory adapter's :import-all similarly re-validates and only skips exact replays, so the Mongo path is a parity gap too. Scope defense fails: this card (ENG-017F) edited :import-all (added the upfront validation in commit 5d9f9e8) and its comment claims 'import-all validates the whole payload before any insert'; import integrity is the card's declared purpose ('import-from-file checks only :format' defect, 'never silently omitted' invariant). The catch line itself is grandfathered from 6924b101, but the card owns this function and the import-integrity story. No comment in the card history documents the blanket swallow as an accepted tradeoff. Consequence is real: non-dup-key write failures (oversized doc, auth/write-concern, driver validation) silently drop records while import-from-file reports manifest-matching counts, since imported-counts are computed from the input payload, not from confirmed inserts. In-scope, undocumented, silent data loss on the exact path this card exists to harden — a legitimate should-fix finding."
    "The code reading is accurate — mongo.clj:789-792 catches MongoWriteException without checking the error code, unlike the 11000-guarded paths at 608-619/629-635 — but the finding does not gate this card for three reasons. (1) Grandfathered code: git log -L shows the catch-all swallow predates ENG-017F (present since commit 1b07a86, carried through 98a522e and d90188a unchanged); ENG-017F (5d9f9e8) added the upfront payload-validation doseq and the decode-validated read paths, and did not introduce or alter the insert loop's exception handling. A pre-existing pattern the card did not touch is not a finding against this card. (2) Out of declared scope: the card's import-side deliverable is 'import-from-file validates all of it BEFORE any mutation' — delivered and test-pinned (7 corruption fixtures fail pre-mutation with state-unchanged proof). The full restore drill, including conflict semantics on re-import, is explicitly listed as a non-goal belonging to ENG-021A ('this card provides the integrity primitives 021A's drill must call'). Dup-key swallowing is also defensible restore-idempotency semantics: re-importing into a partially populated store must not fail. (3) Materially narrowed by the card's own work: every record is schema- and version-validated before any insert, so the realistic driver-side rejection classes the claim cites (document validation, malformed docs) are precluded by construction; remaining cases (oversized doc >16MB, auth/write-concern) are exotic operational failures, not the silent-corruption class the card's invariants target (those invariants are about read-path decode, which is fully covered). A legitimate hardening note for ENG-021A's restore drill — worth a comment or child card — but not a promotion-gating defect in ENG-017F."]}
  {:title
   "unknown-collection corruption fixture never exercises the unknown-collection branch",
   :file "test/epiphany/domain/backup_integrity_test.clj",
