   ["The mechanical reading is accurate — verified against backup.clj: validate-backup-payload runs the content-hash check (lines 102-108) before the per-record doseq (109-111), and the fixture at backup_integrity_test.clj:142-154 keeps the original :content-hash, so the test trips on 'content hash mismatch' rather than validate-record's unknown-collection branch (45-48). But it is not promotion-gating, and a prior skeptic pass in this same review run (.ημ/runs/eng-017f-review/journal.jsonl, verify:unknown-collection...) already refuted it on grounds that independently hold: (1) Self-declared 'nit' severity — the card's acceptance criterion is 'all corruption fixtures fail before any write with state-unchanged proof,' and this fixture does exactly that: an unknown-collection backup fails pre-mutation with :integrity/corrupt and byte-identical state. The invariant is behavioral ('a backup that fails any manifest check mutates nothing'), not line-targeted. (2) The primary guards against unrecognized collections are the manifest-vs-payload collection-set equality (backup.clj:92) and count equality (:97), which sit earlier in the same function; validate-record's guard is layered defense-in-depth, and the fixture is consistent with that design. (3) The reviewer's proposed fix is itself broken: even with :content-hash stripped, the fixture assoc's \"bogus-collection\" as an EMPTY vector, and the doseq destructures over records — zero records means validate-record is never invoked for that key, so the 'fixed' test would import successfully (counts 0 == 0) and fail its own assertion. Exercising the branch would require a non-empty bogus collection plus a stripped/recomputed hash — a different fixture than proposed. A nit-severity test-isolation observation whose suggested remedy doesn't work cannot gate a review→done promotion."
    "The claim's mechanical reading is accurate: validate-backup-payload runs the content-hash check (backup.clj:102-108) before the per-record doseq (backup.clj:109-111), and the unknown-collection fixture keeps the original :content-hash, so the test does trip on hash mismatch rather than on validate-record's unknown-collection branch (backup.clj:45-48). However this does not gate promotion. (1) The claimed severity is itself 'nit', and the test still verifies exactly what the card's acceptance criteria require: an unknown/corrupt collection in a backup fails BEFORE any mutation with :integrity/corrupt and byte-identical state — the card's invariant is 'a backup that fails any manifest check mutates nothing', not 'each fixture must reach a specific line'. (2) The unknown-collection guard in validate-record is a deep secondary check; the primary guards against unrecognized collections (manifest-vs-payload collection-set equality at backup.clj:92 and count equality at :97) sit earlier in the same function and the fixture's behavior is consistent with that layered design. (3) The reviewer's proposed fix is itself wrong: even with :content-hash stripped, the fixture assoc's \"bogus-collection\" as an EMPTY vector, and the doseq destructures [collection-key records] over records — zero records means validate-record is never invoked for that key, so the 'fixed' test would sail through validation, import successfully (counts 0 == 0), and fail its own assertion. Exercising that branch would require a bogus collection containing at least one record — a different fixture than proposed. A real but non-gating coverage refinement; suitable as a follow-up comment, not a review→done blocker."]}]}
